      "id": "bidding-control-flexibility-tradeoff",
      "domain": "bidding",
      "claim": "Tighter cost controls (cost cap, ROAS goal, bid cap) restrict the delivery system's flexibility to find lower-cost opportunities, potentially increasing overall costs; less restrictive strategies let the system capture cheaper high-performing inventory but risk slower delivery, longer learning phases, and incomplete budget spend. Bids should always be set based on the advertiser's willingness to pay for each outcome.",
      "detail": "This is the fundamental tradeoff in Meta's bidding architecture. Lowest cost has maximum flexibility for discovering opportunities but no cost guardrails. Each restrictive control trades delivery speed and volume for cost predictability. This same tradeoff surfaces diagnostically when an ad set shows high CPC/CPM or limited delivery despite sufficient budget while using strict cost controls.",
